<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second French intervention in Mexico</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1861–1867</span> · Also known as French intervention in Mexico</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The second French intervention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> was the invasion of Mexico by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1861 to 1867, launched under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-juarez">Benito Juárez</a> suspended payment of foreign debts. Despite a defeat at Puebla on 5 May 1862, French troops took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> and installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-i-holy-roman-emperor">Maximilian of Habsburg</a> as emperor; after France withdrew, Juárez's republicans captured and executed Maximilian in 1867.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-french-intervention-in-mexico.jpg" alt="Second French intervention in Mexico" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Adolphe Beaucé (1818-1875) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Si%C3%A8ge_de_Puebla_-_29_mars_1863.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
